<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mas'ud I of Ghazni</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 998–1041</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Masud I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Masud I was sultan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire</a>, son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a>. He failed to contain the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> and was decisively defeated by them at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dandanaqan">Battle of Dandanaqan</a> in 1040. The defeat opened the way for the rise of the Seljuk Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/masud-i-of-ghazni.jpg" alt="Mas'ud I of Ghazni" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mas%27udIGhaznavidCoin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mas'ud was born in 998 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a>, the Ghaznavid capital, together with his younger twin brother Mohammad. In 1015 his father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a>, named him heir and made him governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>. He led an expedition into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghor">Ghur</a>, then still a pagan enclave, and took part in his father's campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a>, which annexed the Buyid amirate of Ray. He then directed the Ghaznavid wars in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and forced the Kakuyid ruler Muhammad ibn Rustam Dushmanziyar to recognise Ghaznavid authority.</p><p>Relations with his father were poor, and Mahmud changed his mind and named Mohammad, far less experienced, as heir. When Mahmud died, Mohammad succeeded him, but Mas'ud had the backing of his uncle Yusuf ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabuktigin">Sabuktigin</a> and of the army, including the officer Ali Daya. He marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a>, defeated and imprisoned his brother, who was blinded, and crowned himself sultan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Mas'ud brought the former statesman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-hasan-maymandi">Ahmad Maymandi</a> out of prison to serve as his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, while his old assistant Abu Sahl Zawzani grew powerful at court. Suspicious of his father's officers, he imprisoned his uncle Yusuf and the statesman Ali ibn Il-Arslan. His reign was spent in constant war: against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanids</a>, who killed his governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a>, Altun Tash, at Dabusiyya; against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a>, which he took and lost again; against the rebel Ahmad Inaltigin; and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where he won the Siege of Hansi in 1037. In 1034 he sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amol">Amul</a> for four days and then burned it.</p><p>Meanwhile the Seljuq Turks under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughril">Tughril</a>, who had asked him for asylum, defeated the army he sent against them and won the cession of Nasa, Farava and Dihistan. They went on to take the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, and Tughril proclaimed himself its ruler at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a>. Mas'ud drove them from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> and Nishapur, then marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a> with an army of 50,000 men.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the defeat at Dandanaqan, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a>, Mas'ud left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> to set up his capital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and imprisoned Ali Daya and other generals at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>. His army revolted and restored Mohammad, who confined Mas'ud at Giri, where he was killed on the orders of Mohammad or of Mohammad's son Ahmed. Mas'ud's son Maw'dud later avenged him by killing Mohammad and took the throne; three other sons, Ibrahim, Ali and Farrukh-Zad, also ruled the empire in later years. He is remembered by the title Amir-i Shahid, the martyr king.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Masʽud I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the Battle of Dandanaqan in 1040 Tughril and Chaghri routed the army of the Ghaznavid sultan <b>Masud I</b>. The victory secured Khorasan and opened the Iranian plateau to Seljuk expansion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.55,68.42&amp;year=1025" aria-label="Ghazni on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6298 947 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mas'ud I of Ghazni"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6748 1217h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6748 1217h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.55,68.42&amp;year=1025">What was here in 1025? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
